# Conditions that must last

A single reading can be wrong. Lying on a sensor can press a false low into it
for a few minutes (a "compression low"), and a fresh sensor can be jumpy for its
first day. An alert on every one of those readings teaches people to ignore
alerts.

**Make sustained**, in a row's **...** menu, adds a **for at least N min** box to
the row. The row then only counts once its condition has been true **for that
long without a break**.

<Screenshot id="alert-rule-sustained-low" />

## How the clock works

- The first time the condition is true, a clock starts. At that moment the row
  is **not** yet true.
- Every check after that, the row is true once the clock has reached the
  number of minutes you set.
- The first time the condition is **not** true, the clock is thrown away. The
  next time it is true, the clock starts again from zero.

With a CGM that sends a reading every 5 minutes, "for at least 15 min" means
four readings in a row: the one that starts the clock, and three more, the
last of them 15 minutes after the first.

Here are two rules on a quiet afternoon with a short dip: three readings just
under 70 mg/dL (3.9 mmol/L), covering ten minutes.

<AlertSimulator
  scenario="brief-dip"
  scenarios={["brief-dip", "overnight-low"]}
  rules={["low", "sustained-low"]}
/>

"Low" alerts on the first reading under 70. "Low for 15 minutes" never alerts,
because the dip is over before its clock reaches 15 minutes. Switch to
**Overnight low**, a real low that lasts: now both alert, and "Low for 15
minutes" alerts exactly 15 minutes after the first low reading.

That delay is the price. A sustained rule always alerts later than the same
rule without it, by the number of minutes you set.

<Callout type="warning" title="Do not delay an urgent low">

  A "for at least" setting holds back every alert by its length, including
  real ones. Leave it off rules for dangerous situations, such as an urgent low,
  where a late alert costs more than a false one. A common pattern is two rules:
  an urgent low with no delay, and a milder low with a short one. See
  [Example rules](/docs/alerts/recipes).

</Callout>

## When readings stop

A gap in readings does **not** reset the clock. Only a reading where the
condition is false does.

A glucose rule is checked when a reading arrives. If readings stop while a
clock is running, nothing checks the rule until the next reading. When that
reading arrives and the condition is still true, the time without readings
counts towards the clock, so the rule can alert on the very first reading back.

While readings are missing, glucose rules cannot alert at all. That is why
every setup that relies on alerts should also have a **Signal loss** rule (see
[Conditions](/docs/alerts/conditions#signal-loss)).

<Callout type="info" title="The simulator and Replay fill gaps with the last reading">

  This simulator, and **Replay** in the app, check every 5 minutes whether or
  not a reading arrived, reusing the last reading through a gap. Around a gap,
  a sustained glucose rule can therefore alert earlier in a replay than it
  would have at the time.

</Callout>

## Several conditions that must last

Every row's clock runs on its own, whatever the rows around it are doing, so
the order of rows never changes when a rule alerts.

In the rule editor, **Make sustained** is offered on a single condition row,
not on a group. Two rows of "for at least 15 min" under **all of** alert once
both have held for 15 minutes: after 15 minutes if both started together, later
if one started later. To require that several conditions hold together for a
while, making one of them sustained is usually enough.
